## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical skills in enforcing privacy, security, and auditability in a multi-tenant LLM-ops gateway. It requires concrete architecture and measurable guarantees beyond high-level design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Deterministic validation and replayability
- PII detection and redaction
- Prompt-injection guardrails
- Latency budgeting and tamper-evident logging

## Code Example

```javascript
function validatePrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // normalize
  let p = prompt.trim();
  // redaction rules (pseudo)
  policy.redactionPatterns.forEach(r => p = p.replace(r, 'REDACTED'));
  // injection guard (very simple placeholder)
  const blocked = policy.injectionPatterns.some(pat => new RegExp(pat, 'i').test(p));
  return { valid: !blocked, redacted: p };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy coverage across tenants with different privacy rules?
- How would you handle policy updates without affecting in-flight prompts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cost-aware request routing, a practical constraint in real llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant budgeting and isolation
- Per-model pricing and token estimation
- Atomic budget updates
- Fallback routing and quotas

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for budget check and routing
async function routePrompt(tenant, tokens, model){
  const pricePerK = PRICE[tenant][model];
  const cost = Math.ceil(tokens/1000) * pricePerK;
  const ok = await Redis.decrbyfloat(`budget:${tenant}`, cost);
  if(ok >= 0){
    return forwardToModel(tenant, model);
  } else {
    // revert and fallback
    await Redis.incrbyfloat(`budget:${tenant}`, cost);
    return forwardToBackup(tenant);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for race conditions in budget updates across multiple workers?
- How would you handle dynamic price changes mid-session?

